TOPIC 5 : STEP INTO, STEP OVER & STEP OUT
======================================================================

Q1. What is the difference between Step Into, Step Over, and Step Out?

Interview Ready Answer

Step Into enters the called function and allows the developer to debug its internal execution line by line.

Step Over executes the current line completely without entering any called functions and pauses at the next line in the current function.

Step Out completes the execution of the current function, returns to the calling function, and pauses immediately after the function call.

Definition

Step Into
• Enters the called function.
• Used when the function's internal logic needs to be inspected.

Step Over
• Executes the current line completely.
• Does not enter any called functions.
• Stops at the next line in the current function.

Step Out
• Executes the remaining code of the current function.
• Returns to the calling function.
• Pauses immediately after the function call.

Purpose

Step Into
• Debug the internal implementation of a function.
• Inspect variable values inside the function.

Step Over
• Skip debugging inside a function whose implementation is already trusted.
• Continue debugging the current function efficiently.

Step Out
• Exit the current function quickly.
• Resume debugging in the calling function.

======================================================================

INTERVIEW TRAP QUESTIONS
======================================================================

Q1. Does Step Over enter a function call?

Answer

No.

Step Over executes the function completely without entering it.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q2. Does Step Into always move to the next line?

Answer

No.

If the current line contains a function call, Step Into enters that function.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q3. When should Step Out be used?

Answer

When you have already entered a function and want to finish its execution and return to the caller.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Which command is best for debugging the internal logic of a function?

Answer

Step Into.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q5. Which command is best when you do not want to debug a function's implementation?

Answer

Step Over.

